# Code Style

## 1. Named functions (declaration style)

- Components, helpers, and handlers use `function` declarations, not arrow functions assigned to `const`.
- JSX event props reference a named handler; don't write an inline arrow.
- Hook callbacks (`useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`) are named function expressions so stack traces and React DevTools show a name.
- Allowed exceptions: single-expression callbacks to array methods (`.map`, `.filter`, `.sort`) and state updaters (`setItems((previous) => [...previous, item])`).

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
const handleMeasure = async () => { ... }
const pointsLayerId = (name: string) => `speed-points:${name}`
useEffect(() => { saveReadings(readings) }, [readings])
<select onChange={(event) => setSelectedNetwork(event.target.value)} />

// ✅ GOOD
async function handleMeasure() { ... }
function pointsLayerId(name: string) { return `speed-points:${name}` }
useEffect(function persistReadings() { saveReadings(readings) }, [readings])
function handleNetworkChange(event: ChangeEvent<HTMLSelectElement>) { setSelectedNetwork(event.target.value) }
<select onChange={handleNetworkChange} />
```

## 2. No magic numbers or strings

- Any literal with meaning beyond itself becomes an `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` constant at the top of the module that owns the concept. Export it from that module when other files need it (like `LOCATION_OPTIONS` in `networkReading.ts`). Don't create a catch-all `constants.ts`.
- Put units in the name: `_PX`, `_MS`, `_MBPS`, `_METERS`, `_DECIMALS`.
- A string repeated in more than one place (DOM ids, storage keys, layer ids) is a constant.
- Allowed bare literals: `0`, `1`, `-1` as an identity, index, or increment, and `''` as an empty value.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
reading.latitude.toFixed(5)
<Popup offset={10} />
<input list="known-networks" />  ...  <datalist id="known-networks">

// ✅ GOOD
const COORDINATE_DECIMALS = 5
const POPUP_OFFSET_PX = 10
const KNOWN_NETWORKS_LIST_ID = 'known-networks'
```

## 3. Styling lives in separate files

- Never write `style={{ ... }}` in JSX. Use `className`. The one exception is a continuous runtime value no class can express, like a bar's width percentage; keep everything else about that element in CSS.
- Each component gets its own folder under `src/components/<area>/` (`map`, `panel`, `network`) holding `<Component>.tsx` and `<Component>.css`, e.g. `components/panel/MeasurePanel/MeasurePanel.css` imported by `MeasurePanel.tsx`.
- Only app-wide styles go in `src/styles/global.css`. Colors and font sizes used by more than one component are CSS custom properties in `src/styles/variables.css`; use `var(--color-text-muted)` rather than repeating `#666`.
- Class names are kebab-case and prefixed with the component name: `measure-panel`, `measure-panel-error`.
- Express conditional styling with modifier classes or `data-*` attributes. Don't compute style objects.
- When a library only accepts style objects (react-map-gl `<Map style>`, MapLibre layer `paint`/`layout`), define them as named constants in `<Component>.styles.ts` next to the component and import them.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — in WifiMap.tsx
<Map style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%' }} />
const speedPointsPaint = { 'circle-radius': 5, 'circle-color': ... }

// ✅ GOOD — WifiMap.styles.ts exports MAP_CONTAINER_STYLE, speedPointsPaint
<Map style={MAP_CONTAINER_STYLE} />
```

## 4. React conventions

- One exported component per file. The file name matches the component in PascalCase. Props are a `type <Component>Props`, destructured in the signature.
- Custom hooks are named `useX` and follow the Rules of Hooks: call them at the top level only, never in conditions or loops.
- Derive values during render. Don't mirror props or other state into `useState` + `useEffect`. Use effects only to sync with external systems (storage, map instance, network).
- Model multi-step state as a discriminated union (`{ status: 'idle' } | { status: 'done'; ... }`), not as several booleans.
- Update state immutably. Use the functional updater when the next state depends on the previous one.
- List `key`s are stable ids, never array indexes.
- Business logic and I/O live in `src/lib/`. Components render and wire things together.
- No nested ternaries in JSX. Extract a named variable or a small component instead.
- No `any`, no unused code, no commented-out code, no stray `console.log`.
